{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, longer textual descriptions of the edit operation (greater edit string length) are associated with larger absolute improvements in the task-specific property (|target_delta|).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the complexity of the edit description (measured by edit text length or token count) correlates with the magnitude of the achieved property change in successful examples.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows where target_delta is null.  2. Compute two new columns: edit_length = number of characters in the 'edit' string; edit_tokens = number of whitespace-separated tokens.  3. Compute abs_target_delta = absolute value of target_delta.  4. Calculate Pearson and Spearman correlations between edit_length (and separately edit_tokens) and abs_target_delta.  5. Fit a simple linear regression model predicting abs_target_delta from edit_length (and another from edit_tokens).  6. Plot scatter plots with regression lines for both relationships.  7. Test statistical significance of correlations and regression coefficients.","deliverables":"(a) A table of summary statistics for edit_length, edit_tokens, and abs_target_delta.  (b) Correlation coefficients and p-values (Pearson and Spearman).  (c) Regression model summaries.  (d) Scatter plots with regression lines."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Delete operations have a lower probability of success compared to insert and replace across all optimization tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and test differences in success rates among the three edit types (insert, replace, delete).",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.  2. For each action_type, count successful and unsuccessful examples.  3. Build a contingency table of action_type vs success/failure.  4. Perform a chi-square test of independence to assess whether success is independent of action_type.  5. Compute success rates per action_type.  6. Visualize as a grouped bar chart: success_rate by action_type.","deliverables":"(a) Contingency table and proportions.  (b) Chi-square test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value.  (c) Table of success rates by action_type.  (d) Bar plot comparing success rates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits, increases in molecular weight (MW_delta) are inversely correlated with improvements in drug-likeness (QED_delta), indicating a MW–QED trade-off.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between MW_delta and QED_delta in successful examples to identify a trade-off pattern.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null MW_delta or QED_delta.  2. Compute Pearson and Spearman correlations between MW_delta and QED_delta.  3. Fit a bivariate linear regression model: QED_delta ~ MW_delta.  4. Create a scatter plot of MW_delta vs QED_delta with regression line and confidence interval.  5. Check for heteroskedasticity and nonlinearity.","deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficients and p-values.  (b) Regression model summary (coefficients, R²).  (c) Diagnostic scatter plot with regression line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Retaining the Murcko scaffold constrains the magnitude of molecular weight changes; edits that retain the scaffold exhibit lower variance in MW_delta than those that do not, in both successful and unsuccessful sets.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the variability of MW_delta between scaffold-retained and scaffold-modified edits in both datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null MW_delta or murcko_scaffold_retained.  2. For each dataset, split into two groups by murcko_scaffold_retained (True/False).  3. Compute MW_delta variance and standard deviation for each group.  4. Perform Levene’s test for equality of variances between the two groups in each dataset.  5. Visualize distributions with side-by-side boxplots or violin plots of MW_delta by scaffold retention.","deliverables":"(a) Variance and SD tables for each group and dataset.  (b) Levene’s test statistics and p-values.  (c) Boxplots/violin plots of MW_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Prompts containing explicit functional‐group keywords (e.g., 'hydroxyl', 'amine', 'halogen') lead to more pronounced improvements in synthetic accessibility (more negative SA_delta) than prompts without such keywords.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if specific chemical‐group mentions in the natural language instructions predict larger SA improvements.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; drop rows with null SA_delta.  2. Define a keyword list: ['hydroxyl','amine','halogen','carboxyl','sulfhydryl'].  3. Create a binary feature has_keyword indicating if the prompt contains any keyword (case-insensitive).  4. Split the data into keyword and non-keyword groups.  5. Compare mean SA_delta between groups using a two-sample t-test (or nonparametric Mann–Whitney U test if non-normal).  6. Plot SA_delta distributions for both groups (boxplot/violin plot).","deliverables":"(a) Counts of examples with and without keywords.  (b) Mean and SD of SA_delta per group.  (c) Test statistic and p-value.  (d) Distribution plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Insert operations exhibit greater variability in molecular weight changes (MW_delta) than replace and delete operations in both successful and unsuccessful examples, reflecting more diverse fragment additions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess and compare the spread of MW_delta across action types in both datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null MW_delta or action_type.  2. For each dataset and each action_type, calculate MW_delta variance and interquartile range.  3. Use ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis test to compare variances or distributions across action types for each dataset separately.  4. Visualize with side-by-side boxplots of MW_delta by action_type for successful and unsuccessful sets.","deliverables":"(a) Variance and IQR tables by action_type and dataset.  (b) ANOVA/Kruskal–Wallis test results and p-values.  (c) Comparative boxplots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For the water solubility optimization task (task_id=101), successful edits achieve a larger increase in the number of polar atoms (oxygen + nitrogen) than unsuccessful edits, reflecting effective polarity enhancement.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the change in count of polar atoms between input and output molecules for successful vs unsuccessful edits in task 101.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; filter rows where task_id==101.  2. For each row, parse input_smiles and output_smiles to count occurrences of 'O' and 'N'; compute delta_polar = (O+N)_output – (O+N)_input.  3. Compare mean delta_polar between successful and unsuccessful groups with a t-test or Mann–Whitney U test.  4. Plot both distributions side-by-side.","deliverables":"(a) Summary of mean and SD of delta_polar for both groups.  (b) Test statistic and p-value.  (c) Distribution plots (histogram or violin plot)."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Smaller changes in SMILES string length (|len(output_smiles) – len(input_smiles)|) are predictive of higher edit success likelihood across all tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether the magnitude of SMILES length change is a predictor of edit success.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null SMILES.  2. Compute length_change = absolute difference in string length between output_smiles and input_smiles for each example.  3. Concatenate datasets and create a binary success label (1 for train.parquet, 0 for train_neg.parquet).  4. Fit a logistic regression model predicting success from length_change; optionally include task_id as categorical covariate.  5. Evaluate model performance via ROC curve and report AUC.  6. Plot success rate by binned length_change intervals.","deliverables":"(a) Logistic regression coefficients and p-values.  (b) ROC curve and AUC.  (c) Table of success rates by length_change bins.  (d) Plot of success rate vs length_change."  
    }  
  }  
]}
